# Live transcription

Sanitizer: `functions/src/shared/utils/live-transcript.ts`.

IV-frontend has its own copy at `src/app/shared/utils/live-transcript.ts` plus `live-transcript.spec.ts`. A caption-contract change updates both. Do not assume the files are identical.

## What produces text

| Source | When | Treated as |
|---|---|---|
| Browser caption on `userTurnFinal` | Candidate submits a turn | The answer text Gemini is prompted with |
| `transcribeInterviewAudio` on buffered PCM16 16 kHz | Caption missing, or a live chunk during the open turn | Fallback or interim. Prompt: `TRANSCRIBE_ANSWER_PROMPT` |
| Gemini `outputTranscription` | Interviewer speech | Assistant caption, sanitized before persist and UI |
| Gemini `inputAudioTranscription` | Enabled on the live config (`en-US`) | Candidate PCM is not forwarded to Gemini, so this is not the candidate caption source |

`buildLiveConnectConfig` pins `languageHints` to `en-US`. Keep that pin.

`transcribeInterviewAudio` wraps PCM as WAV, temperature 0, `thinkingBudget: 0` on Gemini 2.x models, and returns `''` on timeout or error. Default timeout is 4.5s. The live chunk path uses 8s. Chunks wait for about 0.8s of new audio and a 600ms debounce (`scheduleLiveChunkTranscription`). Do not transcribe every PCM frame.

The open-turn buffer caps at 90 seconds (`MAX_USER_PCM_BYTES`). Audio shorter than about 0.35s is not sent to the model.

## Partial vs final

- Interim captions use `{ type: 'transcript', role: 'user', text, final: false, live: true }`.
- The submitted answer is `userTurnFinal`. `appendCandidateTurn` persists that text.
- `{ type: 'audio' }` from the client is buffered only. The comment in `live-interview-ws.ts` says Gemini replies once, to the words the candidate saw.

A failed chunk transcription logs a warning and leaves the previous caption. Do not close the socket because one transcription returned `''`.

If `ai-turn.ts` changes, run `npm test` in `functions`. If the sanitizer contract changes, also run the IV-frontend `live-transcript.spec.ts`.
